# SDD §15 — the approved Flatpak application list, installed system-wide at
# first boot by ik-os-firstboot.service.
#
# SDD §54 routes every GUI application here. That makes this file, not the
# package lists, the place where the desktop is defined: the OS image stays a
# minimal GNOME (shell, session, Files, Terminal, Text Editor, Disks) and
# everything a user actually opens is a sandboxed, independently updated app.
#
# Every id must exist on Flathub — it is the only remote the image defines
# (build/scripts/75-flatpak.sh). A typo installs nothing and is only visible in
# the first-boot journal, so build/validation/check-flatpaks.sh resolves each
# line against the Flathub API at validation time.

# The graphical app store, replacing gnome-software.
io.github.kolunmi.Bazaar

# --- Browsers --------------------------------------------------------------
# Browsers are the clearest case for §54: the most exposed application on the
# machine, sandboxed, and updated independently of the image release cadence.

# Mozilla's own build — publisher-verified on Flathub, MPL-2.0.
# Also the default handler for http/https (config/desktop/mimeapps.list).
org.mozilla.firefox

# Google Chrome. Two things worth knowing, neither of them a blocker:
#
#   1. The Flathub packaging is community-maintained, NOT publisher-verified.
#      What it ships, however, is Google's own signed .deb, pulled from
#      dl.google.com against a pinned sha256 and run under the zypak sandbox
#      shim — so the binary is first-party even though the wrapper is not.
#   2. Being an "extra-data" package, installing it downloads that .deb from
#      Google at install time. First boot therefore needs dl.google.com
#      reachable, not just Flathub; and when Google rotates the stable version
#      out of its pool, the pinned URL 404s until Flathub bumps the manifest.
#      ik-os-firstboot retries and logs a failure per app rather than silently
#      stamping the step done, so this surfaces instead of hiding.
com.google.Chrome

# Chromium-based third browser for testing and for sites that dislike Firefox.
com.brave.Browser

# --- Communication and PIM -------------------------------------------------

# Unofficial Teams client (Microsoft ships no native Linux app). It lives in the
# tray, which works because packages/desktop/extensions.list enables the
# appindicator extension.
com.github.IsmaelMartinez.teams_for_linux

# Evolution is here for Exchange: it is the only Linux client with working EWS
# calendar and address-book support.
org.gnome.Evolution
org.gnome.Calendar
org.gnome.Contacts

# --- Remote access ---------------------------------------------------------
com.rustdesk.RustDesk
org.gnome.Connections

# Thincast pulls org.kde.Platform 5.15 (~975 MB) — see the runtime note at the
# bottom of this file.
com.thincast.client

# --- Secrets and privacy ---------------------------------------------------
# KeePassXC is the Qt application here, and also pulls org.kde.Platform 5.15.
org.keepassxc.KeePassXC
org.gnome.World.Secrets
com.belmoussaoui.Obfuscate
com.belmoussaoui.Decoder

